Bytecode that refers to its script's text; smaller parse buffers.

Two changes for pages whose documents load the same large script.

JS_WRITE_OBJ_SOURCE_REF writes, for each function, where its text lies
in the script's text (patch 0008) instead of the text itself, and
JS_ReadObjectSource reads such bytecode given the script again: the
functions it makes point into the one text of that script, which the
runtime shares between contexts. The embedder can then compile a script
once and give other contexts of the page its bytecode, with
Function.prototype.toString unchanged. Writing fails for a function
whose text is not part of a whole script's text (a script that is
mostly data). Measured on the host with an 860 KB script: compiling
took 116 ms and 4 MB of working memory beyond what it keeps; writing
the bytecode (1.2 MB) took 3 ms and reading it 3 ms with no working
memory.

js_fd_trim gives back the unused part of a function's first-pass
bytecode buffer when the function has been parsed. The buffers stay
until the whole script is parsed and grow by half each time: for the
same script 0.7 MB of 3.4 MB was unused.

